.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "September 2026" "conn 0.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- every open project and its processes, in one window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn ls
.br
.B conn restart
.br
.B conn \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A terminal UI. It lists each open project's shells, dev servers and agents, and
marks the ones waiting for input. macOS and Linux. Processes keep running when
the window is closed.
.SH INSTALLATION
.TP
1-1.
conn requires \fBtmux\fR (holds the shells) and \fBlsof\fR (reads the process
list). macOS ships lsof; install tmux with \fBbrew install tmux\fR. On Linux,
install both from the package manager.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ curl -fsSL https://conn.w0zro.com/install.sh | sh
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-2.
The script downloads the release for this machine, verifies it against
\fBchecksums.txt\fR, and installs the binary to \fB~/.local/bin\fR and the
manpage under \fB~/.local/share/man\fR. \fBman conn\fR is the full reference.
Three variables override the script:
.PP
.I "Table 1-1. Install script overrides"
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_INSTALL_DIR\fR
alternate install directory
.TP
\fBCONN_MAN_DIR\fR
alternate manpage directory
.TP
\fBCONN_VERSION\fR
install a specific release
.RE
.TP
1-3.
Building from source requires Go 1.25 or later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ go install github.com/w0zro/conn@latest
.fi
.RE
.SH FIRST RUN
.RS
.nf
$ conn
.fi
.RE
.TP
2-1.
There is nothing to set up. \fBconn\fR brings up a tmux server of its own,
attaches the terminal to it, and opens the navigator down the left of the
session's first window. The status line at the foot is tmux's, and names the
mode the keys are in. conn looks for repositories under \fB~/projects\fR; the
configuration (Section 8) points it elsewhere.
.TP
2-2.
The list opens showing what is running, so on a quiet machine it may say
\fBnothing running\fR. Two keys from there:
.PP
.I "Table 2-1. First keys"
.RS
.TP
\fB.\fR
show every repository, running or not
.TP
\fB/\fR
find one by name
.RE
.SH THE NAVIGATOR
.TP
3-1.
The left column is a tree, at most four levels deep: \fBgroup\fR →
\fBrepository\fR → \fBsub\-project\fR → \fBprocess\fR. A repository is every
git repository under the project roots. The scan skips \fBnode_modules\fR,
\fBvendor\fR, \fBtarget\fR, \fB.build\fR, \fBPods\fR, \fB.venv\fR and
\fBvenv\fR, every directory whose name begins with a dot, and anything named in
\fBskipDirs\fR.
.TP
3-2.
A folder inside a root that holds two or more repositories groups them. The
group is a place of its own: \fBs\fR opens a shell at the folder, \fBr\fR runs
the folder's plan, \fBx\fR covers everything beneath it.
.TP
3-3.
In a monorepo, a directory with a manifest is a project of its own and gets a
row under its repository. Manifests, in the order they are trusted: \fB.conn\fR,
\fBProcfile\fR, \fBpackage.json\fR, \fBdeno.json\fR, \fBcomposer.json\fR,
\fBgo.mod\fR, \fBCargo.toml\fR, \fBGemfile\fR, \fBmix.exs\fR,
\fBpyproject.toml\fR, \fBpom.xml\fR, \fBbuild.gradle\fR, \fBbuild.gradle.kts\fR.
Ignored directories are skipped, and an untracked \fB.conn\fR still counts.
.TP
3-4.
Each place lists the processes running in it, attributed to the innermost place
they belong to, and named by what you would call them — \fBnpm run dev\fR, not
\fBnode\fR — with the port beside a row that listens, \fBnpm run dev ·
:5173\fR, the way an agent's row carries its model. A shell a plan or a task
started is named for its entry, whatever runs in it: \fBweb\fR, not the
http.server that is web this time. Keys that shape the list:
.PP
.I "Table 3-1. Shaping the list"
.RS
.TP
\fBspace\fR
fold or unfold the selected node; a folded node shows +n for what it hides
.TP
\fB\-\fR
fold or unfold everything; unfolded rows also show pids
.TP
\fB.\fR
toggle between everything and only what is running
.RE
.TP
3-5.
Dim rows are processes with no shell of their own to go to: those started
outside conn, and those a process in a shell started in turn — a claude's
tools, say — which \fBenter\fR reaches only as far as the shell above them.
All are listed and killable. One of them may be conn itself — the launcher
become a tmux client, in the terminal it was started from — and that row says
\fB(me)\fR in a color of its own. A row's mark says how its command ended, or
that its process is unwell:
.PP
.I "Table 3-2. Row marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB✗\fR
in red: the command ended badly, or the process is stopped, in an
uninterruptible wait, or a zombie. Beside the name, what the run said of itself
and how long ago: \fBtest · 3 failed · 3m\fR
.TP
\fB✓\fR
in green: the command ended well — \fBtest · 12 passed · 2h\fR
.RE
.TP
3-6.
Beside the list, while the keys are in it, is the navigator's own pane: what is
known about the row under the cursor, a held shell's included, with the last of
what that shell has shown beneath the facts, in the colors the shell drew it in.
What a process is doing can be read without entering it. A shell is placed
beside the navigator only when you enter it, and goes back to a window of its
own when the keys come home.
.TP
3-7.
\fB/\fR finds places and processes: a query lists the processes that answer it
— by anything their row says: the command, what it was run with, the plan's
name for it, an agent's name and model, a port — under the places they run,
pruned to the branches that answer. An empty query lists places alone. Up and
down, or \fBctrl\-p\fR and \fBctrl\-n\fR, move the cursor through what is left,
and acting on what it is on ends the looking: \fBenter\fR opens a shell on a
place and steps into a found process's shell, \fBctrl\-r\fR runs the plan there,
\fBctrl\-a\fR starts an agent, \fBtab\fR goes to the next thing that needs you,
\fBshift+tab\fR back where the keys were, and \fBctrl\-x\fR asks to kill what
the cursor is on — the filter holds, so the subject stays listed. \fBesc\fR
abandons the look and puts the cursor back on the row it left. The query takes
readline's editing keys — \fBctrl\-w\fR, \fBctrl\-u\fR, \fBctrl\-h\fR,
\fBctrl\-e\fR and the rest.
.TP
3-8.
Matching is fuzzy: each word of the query appears in order, not contiguously —
\fBtsl\fR finds tressle-api, \fBmono api\fR finds the api inside mono — and
the letters that matched are lit in the rows.
.TP
3-9.
\fBn\fR makes a new project. It asks where on the status line, on a line
prefilled with the folder the cursor is in relative to its root — \fBw0zro/\fR
for a cursor on a repository in that folder, nothing for one at the root — and
the name goes on the end: backspace the folder away for the root itself, or type
another folder, which is made if it is not there. A path from \fB~\fR or \fB/\fR
is taken as it is. \fBenter\fR makes the directory, runs \fBgit init\fR in it,
and opens a shell there; the cursor lands on the project once the scan lists it.
A directory already there is left alone, and \fBesc\fR thinks better of it.
.SH SHELLS
.TP
4-1.
\fBs\fR opens a shell at the selected place, beside the navigator, and puts the
keys in it. \fBenter\fR on a shell's row does the same for that shell. From then
on every keystroke is the shell's: the pane is tmux's, and it behaves the way a
tmux pane behaves, with the navigator still down the left. conn keeps one key:
\fBctrl\-space\fR, the prefix — tmux's own, bound by conn's configuration —
which reaches conn from any shell. \fBctrl\-space \-\fR is back to the
navigator; \fBctrl\-space j\fR and \fBk\fR are the next and previous shell;
\fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR is back where the keys were — the shell before
this one, whether or not the navigator was the way there, or the navigator when
they came from there; again returns. \fBctrl\-space q\fR leaves, and \fB?\fR —
in the navigator, or after the prefix from any shell — lists the rest in a
popup over the whole window. Every key tmux would otherwise bind is unbound.
.TP
4-2.
A shell you enter moves in beside the navigator; when the keys come home it
moves out again, to a tmux window of its own, where it keeps running, and the
navigator has the whole window. \fBJ\fR and \fBK\fR step through the shells in
the list's order, keys and all.
.TP
4-3.
The status line at the foot is tmux's. It begins with conn's own name,
\fBCONN\fR, in purple, which stays. To its right is the mode the keys are in —
\fBNAV\fR or \fBPROC\fR for the pane that has them, each a chip in its own color
with the line washed one tone after it; \fBPREFIX\fR while a chord hangs;
\fBCOPY\fR in copy mode; the query while one is typed and the filter while one
stands; \fBCONTINUE\fR with the picker open; \fBNEW\fR with a project's name
being typed; \fBCONFIRM\fR while a kill waits on its second key — and then
anything the navigator has to say: a report, an error, a confirmation, until the
next key, or that a newer conn is out, whenever nothing else is said. What a
chord says, from any shell, shows there too, for a few seconds. The right corner
names the kind of agent \fBa\fR starts.
.TP
4-4.
Shells keep running when the terminal closes. \fBq\fR in the navigator, or
\fBctrl\-space q\fR anywhere, detaches; quit the terminal, come back, run
\fBconn\fR, and everything is where it was.
.TP
4-5.
The mouse is tmux's, with tmux's own bindings and nothing of conn's: the wheel
scrolls back through a pane's transcript — each shell keeps 10,000 lines of it
by default, and \fBscrollback\fR in the configuration raises that — a drag
selects and copies to the clipboard on release, a click takes the keys to the
pane under it, and a program that speaks mouse gets its own clicks and wheel.
Hold shift to drag a selection the terminal's own way. A program that copies for
itself — vim's clipboard yank, anything speaking OSC 52 — reaches the system
clipboard through tmux.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A click into the navigator brings the keys home the way \fBctrl\-space \-\fR
does: the shell beside it goes back to its own window, and the navigator's pane
returns.
.SH AGENTS
.TP
5-1.
\fBa\fR starts an agent in the selected place, in a shell like any other —
Claude Code by default; the configuration's \fBagent\fR setting selects another.
Claude Code reports its state, shown as a mark in the navigator:
.PP
.I "Table 5-1. Agent marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB⠇\fR
turning: the instance is working
.TP
\fB◆\fR
blocked mid-turn on something specific: a permission prompt, a question
.TP
\fB●\fR
finished its turn; waiting for what you ask next
.TP
\fB○\fR
idle since it started
.RE
.TP
5-2.
The mark tells you, from across the list, which projects need attention — the
◆ most of all, since answering it resumes work already underway. The pane for
a Claude row names what a blocked instance is waiting on, the last thing asked
of it, and what it says it is doing.
.TP
5-3.
\fBctrl\-space enter\fR goes to the next thing that needs you, from any shell:
an agent waiting, a command that ended badly, a process stopped or a zombie.
Pressing it again continues around them in turn; \fBtab\fR does the same at the
list. A held shell is entered, where the ask or the transcript is; a process
conn only watches gets the cursor.
.TP
5-4.
A conversation does not end when its instance exits; it is suspended, and the
transcript is enough to pick it back up. \fBA\fR on a place lists what is
suspended there — sub-projects included — newest first: when each
conversation last moved, its branch, and the last thing you asked of it. Letters
narrow the list the way the filter does, \fBenter\fR continues the one under the
cursor in a shell opened where the conversation was had, and \fBesc\fR closes
the list. A conversation an instance is still carrying is not offered. Every
kind that keeps conversations answers the picker, and each is continued by the
kind that had it.
.TP
5-5.
Other kinds show as plain processes, unmarked. Ollama runs the model most
recently pulled or run — read from the manifests under \fB~/.ollama/models\fR,
or \fB$OLLAMA_MODELS\fR, so the pick needs no daemon, though the REPL does and
says so when it is not up — unless \fBagentRuns\fR names one. \fB,\fR at the
navigator, or \fBctrl\-space ,\fR from any shell, moves \fBa\fR on to the next
kind — claude, ollama, claude — for the whole server: every \fBa\fR,
\fBctrl\-a\fR and agent chord starts it, the status line's right corner names
it, and it holds until the server goes, when the configuration's kind is back.
.SH PLANS AND TASKS
.TP
6-1.
Working on a project usually means the same processes every time: the dev
server, the test watcher, an agent. A project lists them in \fB.conn\fR —
Procfile format, one name and command per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dev:   npm run dev
api:   go run ./cmd/api
agent: claude
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-1. A plan, in Procfile format"
.TP
6-2.
Without one, conn reads the conventions in this order: a \fBProcfile\fR means
every line is a process; a \fBpackage.json\fR contributes only its \fBdev\fR or
\fBstart\fR script, whichever exists first.
.TP
6-3.
\fBr\fR starts the plan's entries that are not running, each in its own window
named for its entry, and puts the cursor on the first of them. It is a list to
run, not a supervisor: nothing restarts on its own. When an entry's command
ends, its shell stays at the prompt with the transcript: the row wears ✗ in
red if the command ended badly and ✓ in green if it ended well, the checklist
in the place's pane shows the entry down with how and when it ended, and \fBr\fR
starts it again beside the old shell. Running anything in that shell by hand
clears its mark: the ending was acted on.
.TP
6-4.
\fBt\fR, \fBb\fR and \fBl\fR run the place's tests, its build and its lint, the
way the place already says they run. What the project wrote down for the purpose
comes first; otherwise the ecosystem's own command, by the file that marks the
project. Only a linter the ecosystem ships with is guessed at; a project on ruff
or eslint names it in a script. A project the guess is wrong for says so in its
Makefile, which conn believes first.
.PP
.I "Table 6-1. How a task's command is found"
.RS
.TP
\fBMakefile, justfile, Taskfile\fR
the target, recipe or task named \fBtest\fR, \fBbuild\fR or \fBlint\fR
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
the script named for the task, run with npm
.TP
\fBgo.mod\fR
\fBgo test ./...\fR, \fBgo build ./...\fR, \fBgo vet ./...\fR
.TP
\fBCargo.toml\fR
\fBcargo test\fR, \fBcargo build\fR, \fBcargo clippy\fR
.TP
\fBpyproject.toml\fR
\fBpytest\fR; \fBpython3 manage.py test\fR for a Django project
.TP
\fBGemfile\fR
\fBrspec\fR with a spec directory, else \fBrake test\fR
.TP
\fBmix.exs and the rest\fR
the ecosystem's own commands: mix, gradle, mvn, deno, swift, dune
.RE
.TP
6-5.
Each run is a shell named for its task, so its row wears the check when it ends
well and the cross when it fails, \fBtab\fR goes to a failure, and the place's
pane says how each task's last run went and how long ago. The key again replaces
a finished run rather than stacking another beside it; a run still going is left
to finish. \fBconn test\fR, \fBconn build\fR, \fBconn lint\fR and \fBctrl\-space
t\fR, \fBb\fR, \fBl\fR do the same from any shell.
.TP
6-6.
conn reads the end of the transcript the way you would, by the shape of the
tool's summary line — go test, pytest, jest, vitest, mocha, cargo, rspec,
ExUnit, golangci-lint, tsc, ruff, eslint — so a failed run's row says \fBtest
· 3 failed\fR and a passed one \fB12 passed\fR. A shape it does not know leaves
the status alone.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A plan is not a supervisor; nothing restarts automatically. An ended entry's
shell stays at its prompt, with its transcript, until \fBx\fR closes it or
\fBr\fR starts the entry again beside it.
.TP
6-7.
A project on docker runs its services in containers, and a container is not a
process the scan can see: on macOS it runs in docker's own machine, and on Linux
it is root's, working in a directory of its own. docker knows, and compose
writes on every container it starts the directory it was started for — the
same fact lsof reports of a process — so a container is filed under its place
by the rule everything else is, in a row named for its service with the ports it
publishes on the host: \fBweb · :8438\fR. It sits under the \fBdocker compose
up\fR that runs it when one runs in a shell, and under the place itself when
compose was left detached. The row has no shell to enter; its pane says the
container's name, image and status, with the last of its logs, and \fBx\fR asks
docker to stop it. What \fBx\fR stopped, \fBr\fR brings back: the services the
place's compose declares are what compose says it needs, each an entry of the
plan's — \fBdocker compose up\fR for it, named for it — and \fBr\fR starts
the ones that are down the way it starts the plan's, in a window of its own:
compose up again, for the one service, attaches to it the way the first did, so
its logs are in its pane and its container is under it. Unless an entry \fBr\fR
is starting runs compose itself, which brings up everything at once. A container
without a directory — started by hand, or by something other than compose —
belongs to no place and is not listed, the way a process working outside every
project is not. Without docker, or with its daemon down, nothing is asked.
.PP
.RS
.nf
compose-demo
   app
     cache · :6390
     web · :8438
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-2. A compose project's containers, under the compose that runs them"
.SH KILLING
.I "Table 7-1. Kill keys"
.RS
.TP
\fBx\fR
kill the selected process
.TP
\fBX\fR
kill it and everything under it
.TP
\fBx on a repository or group\fR
kill everything running in it
.RE
.TP
7-1.
Every kill asks first — \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR or \fBenter\fR confirms; anything
else cancels. Processes get SIGTERM, and a signalled row wears a red marker
until a rescan actually observes the exit; the list never claims an exit it has
not seen. Kills work on any listed process, including ones conn did not start. A
shell conn holds is closed through the server — but one still running its
command is closed only once the command has gone: what runs in the shell gets
SIGTERM first, and the shell goes when a scan finds it back at its prompt, so a
\fBdocker compose up\fR stops its containers on the way out, which hung up it
would not.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
8-1.
One JSON file, entirely optional, at \fB~/.config/conn/config.json\fR;
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is honored.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  \(dqprojectsDirs\(dq: [\(dq~/projects\(dq, \(dq~/work/mono\(dq],
  \(dqscrollback\(dq: 50000,
  \(dqnavWidth\(dq: 34,
  \(dqskipDirs\(dq: [\(dqbazel-out\(dq, \(dqdist\(dq]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 8-1. A configuration"
.TP
8-2.
Every field has a default, and a file that names none is the same as no file.
Table 8-1 lists them.
.PP
.I "Table 8-1. Configuration fields"
.RS
.TP
\fBprojectsDir\fR
the directory searched for repositories; ~ and $VARS expand (default
$HOME/projects)
.TP
\fBprojectsDirs\fR
several roots; when set, projectsDir is ignored. A root that only exists on
another machine is fine
.TP
\fBscrollback\fR
transcript lines each shell keeps. Written into the server's configuration at
launch, so raising it takes a fresh server: \fBR\fR, then \fBconn\fR again, or
\fBconn restart\fR (default 10000)
.TP
\fBnavWidth\fR
the navigator column's width, 16–60 (default 30)
.TP
\fBtheme\fR
the side tmux draws for, dark or light; the navigator draws with the terminal's
own sixteen colors (default dark)
.TP
\fBagent\fR
the kind of agent \fBa\fR starts, until \fB,\fR says otherwise: claude or ollama
(default claude)
.TP
\fBagentRuns\fR
what starting a kind runs, by name: {\(dqollama\(dq: \(dqollama run mistral\(dq}
.TP
\fBskipDirs\fR
directory names the scan never enters, on top of the built-in list
.RE
.SH THE SERVER
.TP
9-1.
The shells live in a tmux server of conn's own, one per user for all projects,
on a socket at \fB~/.local/state/conn/tmux\-<uid>.sock\fR; \fBCONN_SOCKET\fR
overrides, and \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR moves the directory. Every \fBconn\fR writes
the server's configuration — the prefix and its chords, the status line, the
transcript cap — to \fB~/.local/state/conn/tmux.conf\fR, brings the server up
under it or sources it into one already running, and attaches. No .tmux.conf
reaches it. The session's first window is the home window: the navigator in the
pane down its left, at the configuration's \fBnavWidth\fR through every resize,
and the shell the keys are in beside it; with the keys in the navigator, the
pane is its own, and every shell waits in a window of its own.
.TP
9-2.
Close the navigator and \fBctrl\-space \-\fR makes it again, on the left. A
\fBconn\fR from a newer build than the navigator's restarts the navigator in its
pane, the way it re-reads the configuration: the server keeps the shells, and
everything else is the build's. Run from a shell inside the window, it does that
and no more, the terminal being attached already; inside any other tmux it
refuses, since attaching would be nesting.
.TP
9-3.
The chords run \fBconn\fR itself with a word, each a short command against the
server. None of them are for typing, but all of them can be:
.PP
.I "Table 9-1. Commands against the server"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn home [key]\fR
to the navigator, pressing key there
.TP
\fBconn shell [dir]\fR
a shell in dir, shown beside the navigator
.TP
\fBconn agent [dir]\fR
an agent in dir, shown beside the navigator
.TP
\fBconn kind\fR
the next kind of agent, for \fBa\fR and the agent chord
.TP
\fBconn run [dir]\fR
the plan of the place holding dir
.TP
\fBconn test [dir]\fR
its tests; \fBbuild\fR and \fBlint\fR the same way
.TP
\fBconn jump\fR
the next thing that needs you
.TP
\fBconn back\fR
back where the keys were: the last shell, or the navigator
.TP
\fBconn next, prev\fR
the next and previous shell
.TP
\fBconn keys [client]\fR
the keys, in a popup over the client; \fBconn page\fR is the page inside it
.TP
\fBconn ls\fR
the held shells: pid, directory, name, exit; one per line
.TP
\fBconn restart\fR
the server ended, after asking, and brought up again under this build
.RE
.TP
9-4.
conn keeps itself current. The navigator asks github which release is latest —
on its first paint and once a day after, a plain request for the release page's
redirect, the answer kept in \fB~/.local/state/conn/latest\-release\fR so a
navigator started again is not another ask — and a release newer than the
build says so on the status line whenever nothing else is being said: \fBconn
0.5.0 is out · U installs it\fR. \fBU\fR runs the installer in a popup over the
window, aimed at the directory this build runs from, and then \fBconn\fR, which
gives the server the new configuration and brings the navigator back as the new
build; the shells stay. With nothing newer known, \fBU\fR asks now and says what
it finds. A build that is no release — a tree past a tag, a plain go build —
is never behind one. Upgrading by hand does not touch the server either: install
the new binary, and the next \fBconn\fR sources the new configuration into the
same server and replaces the navigator when the build changed. \fBR\fR ends the
server and every shell it holds, after asking; it is the way to a fresh server
when one is wanted. \fBconn restart\fR, typed at a terminal outside the window,
is \fBR\fR and then \fBconn\fR as one command; inside the window it refuses,
since the pane running it would go with the server. \fBtmux \-S
~/.local/state/conn/tmux\-<uid>.sock attach\fR is what \fBconn\fR does.
.TP
9-5.
\fBconn ls\fR is the same state for scripts. The exit is how the command a shell
was started with ended, and is empty while it runs. No server listening is an
empty list, not an error.
.SH KEYS
.I "Table A-1. In the navigator"
.RS
.TP
\fB↑↓ j k\fR
move
.TP
\fBJ K\fR
next · previous shell
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open
.TP
\fBtab\fR
the next thing that needs you
.TP
\fBshift+tab\fR
back where the keys were
.TP
\fBs\fR
shell
.TP
\fBa\fR
agent
.TP
\fBA\fR
continue a conversation
.TP
\fB,\fR
the next kind of agent
.TP
